  "excerpt": "Lekach is the traditional Ashkenazi honey cake eaten at Rosh Hashanah (the Jewish New Year) to symbolise a sweet new year. It is a dark, moist, generously spiced loaf cake made with honey, oil, and strong black tea, which deepens the colour and adds a subtle complexity. The cake improves markedly over the two to three days after baking, so it is worth making ahead.",
